In C, you can terminate a program several ways depending on the situation:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    return 0;   // Normal successful termination
}
```

Other common options:

```c
exit(0);       // Normal termination; runs atexit handlers and flushes streams
exit(1);       // Terminate with an error status
```

For abnormal, immediate termination:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

abort();       // Raises SIGABRT; typically produces a crash/core dump
```

If you are inside `main`, `return 0;` is usually the cleanest play. A status of `0` signals success; nonzero generally signals an error to the operating system or calling script.

Example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    if (/* something went wrong */ 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error occurred\n");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Finished successfully.\n");
    return 0;
}
```
